  <h2>Where AI answers go wrong</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Invented references:</strong> real-looking citations to papers that don't exist, the classic <a href={href('glossary/hallucination/')}>hallucination</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Misattribution:</strong> a real claim, credited to the wrong paper.</li>
    <li><strong>Overstatement:</strong> a hedged finding reported as certain, or limitations dropped.</li>
    <li><strong>Missing context:</strong> an answer from one section that another section contradicts.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>A five-step check</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Ask for the exact passage and page the answer relies on.</li>
    <li>Open the paper and read that passage and the paragraph around it.</li>
    <li>Verify every reference: title in a database, <a href={href('glossary/doi/')}>DOI</a> resolving to the right paper.</li>
    <li>Check numbers against the table or figure they came from.</li>
    <li>Keep what you checked, with the passage, and mark what the model wrote.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>What makes a tool trustworthy</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>It answers from documents you provide, not from memory (<a href={href('glossary/rag/')}>RAG</a>).</li>
    <li>Each claim links to a passage you can open.</li>
    <li>It says when the documents don't contain the answer.</li>
    <li>AI text is labelled and stored apart from yours.</li>
  </ul>
